{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261118T215953Z-509905bd", "ts": "2026-11-18T22:08:40Z", "actor": {"worktree": "arrt", "plugin": "3.8.2-dev.1", "branch": "ci/testament-pool", "build": "3682719168c6"}, "body": {"base_commit": "42ce116dbebbe79ef19d86c9f098efe6e2fb7f40", "base_tree": "a0262fed92cbdfb68b2a1deef96f779e17b9b8e5", "head_tree": "4ec26b40a844c98dbb153d53e585b6f8bde7ef77", "head_commit": "0ff950e2095727b9b0a59cf023c695ec31b27703", "dispatch_commit": "0ff950e2095727b9b0a59cf023c695ec31b27703", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": [".github/scripts/assert_tests_ran.py", ".github/scripts/browser_shard.py", ".github/workflows/api-drift.yml", ".github/workflows/browser.yml", ".github/workflows/drift-freshness.yml", ".github/workflows/image.yml", ".github/workflows/suites.yml", ".github/workflows/tests.yml", "deploy/README.md", "docs/testing.md", "tests/test_assert_tests_ran.py", "tests/test_browser_shards.py", "tests/test_default_suite_ci_scope.py"], "files_changed": [".github/scripts/assert_tests_ran.py", ".github/scripts/browser_shard.py", ".github/workflows/api-drift.yml", ".github/workflows/browser.yml", ".github/workflows/drift-freshness.yml", ".github/workflows/image.yml", ".github/workflows/suites.yml", ".github/workflows/tests.yml", ".prawduct/artifacts/boundary-patterns.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-ci-on-testament.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-server-image.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/observability-strategy.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/project-preferences.md", ".prawduct/changes/ci-on-testament.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md", ".prawduct/project-state.yaml", "deploy/README.md", "docs/testing.md", "tests/test_assert_tests_ran.py", "tests/test_browser_shards.py", "tests/test_default_suite_ci_scope.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/boundary-patterns.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-ci-on-testament.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-server-image.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/observability-strategy.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/project-preferences.md", ".prawduct/changes/ci-on-testament.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md", ".prawduct/project-state.yaml"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "blocking", "title": "tests.yml's lint step swallows a root ruff failure: a non-final command of an && list never trips bash -e", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Why it broke: GitHub runs an unspecified `run:` as `bash -e {0}`, and bash -e does not exit when a command that is not the last of an && list fails. Line 65 of .github/workflows/tests.yml is `uv run ruff check . && uv run black --check .`. If ruff fails, black is skipped, the shell carries on, and the step's status is whatever line 67 (the contract subshell) returns. So a ruff violation in the root project's files (tests/, .github/scripts/; the root config excludes arrt/ and contract/) passes CI whenever the two planes lint clean. The subshell lines 66-67 still fail correctly, because each subshell's last command is the failed list. The retired suites.yml ran each command on a line of its own, so this is a regression, and it removes CI enforcement of the norm-index rows project-preferences.md assigns to ruff at the root (T20 with the .github/scripts carve-out, E/F, I, UP, B, TRY, N, BLE). The same shape is at line 87, `[ -n \"$name\" ] && [ -n \"$want\" ]`: an empty `$name` does not stop the step there. The step happens to fail later, at `mv`, with a misleading message. Fix: give every command its own line, or turn each list into `a && b || exit 1`. Bound the class by grepping .github/workflows/*.yml for `&&` inside `run:` blocks that is not on the block's last line; today there are three members (65, 87, and 115, which sits inside an `if` test and is fine). Cheapest mechanism: a check in tests/test_default_suite_ci_scope.py that refuses a non-final `&&` list in any `run:` line. Then watch it fail once against today's line 65 (core.md: 'When a test, lint or check is new or rewritten, watch it fail once').", "files": [".github/workflows/tests.yml"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "The narrowing guard refuses `--suite curation` but passes `--suite=curation`, `--area=`, `--not-area=` and `--changed-since=`", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Why it broke: `check_the_pool_run` keeps a denylist of narrowing spellings, but testament's `_split_run_args` (cli.py) accepts `--name=value` for every valued flag. `\"--suite\" not in args` therefore misses `--suite=curation`, and the selection scan skips any dash-prefixed token. As a result `testament run --json=summary --suite=curation --junit-xml \"$OUT/all.xml\" --no-fallback` passes `test_ci_runs_every_suite_on_the_pool_and_guards_its_report`, and so do `--area=X`, `--not-area=X` and `--changed-since=REF`. Each of these runs less than every suite while CI stays green, which is the failure the module docstring says the test prevents. The space-separated forms of --area, --changed-since and --manifest are refused only by accident: their value gets counted as a path selection, and the message then blames a path. Fix: replace the denylist with an allowlist of the flags the run may carry (`--json[=summary]`, `--junit-xml PATH`, `--no-fallback`, and any you add on purpose), and refuse every other token by name. Add a refused case for `--suite=curation`. This is core.md's 'When you fix a defect, make inputs the fix does not know fail by name'.", "files": ["tests/test_default_suite_ci_scope.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "`a && b` used as a must-pass sequence under the runner's `bash -e`: a root-level ruff failure passes the lint step", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Why it broke: the old lint job put each command on its own line, and folding them into `a && b` meets the errexit exemption, which ignores a failure in any element of an `&&` list except the last. In tests.yml's lint step, line 1 is `uv run ruff check . && uv run black --check .`. If ruff fails, the list short-circuits, `bash -e` does not exit, and the step's status comes from the last line (the contract lint). So a ruff error at the governance root merges green. Nothing else lints in CI, because the pool runs only pytest. Lines 2 and 3 are safe only because each subshell's non-zero exit is a plain command failure. A second member is in the install step, `[ -n \"$name\" ] && [ -n \"$want\" ]`: an empty `name` is swallowed and the step fails later at `mv` with an unrelated message. Fix: put each command on its own line (or `set -e` plus a sequence), and write the header assertion as `[ -n \"$name\" ] || { echo ::error::...; exit 1; }`. Bound of the class: search every `run:` block in .github/workflows for a top-level `&&` whose left side is a check meant to fail the step. To close the class rather than these two, the `_run_lines` helper in tests/test_default_suite_ci_scope.py could refuse a top-level `&&` outside a subshell. Correctness may rate the lint hole under Goal 1.", "files": [".github/workflows/tests.yml"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "CLAUDE.md still says its command table is what CI runs, and since this change CI runs `.testament.toml`'s commands on the pool", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Why it broke: tests.yml replaced the per-suite `uv run pytest` jobs, but CLAUDE.md (lines 41-42, outside the diff) still says \"The commands above are what CI runs; testament's own fallback runs `.testament.toml`'s commands here.\" CI now runs `testament run --no-fallback`, which uses `.testament.toml`'s commands (curation at `-n 0`, browser by marker, `--rootdir=.`). A maintainer reproducing a red CI run from this table runs a different command (curation under `-n auto`, no browser suite) and can get a different answer. Make the sentence relational: say CI runs the lint rows directly and every suite through `testament run` (tests.yml), as `.testament.toml` declares. Do not restate the commands.", "files": [".github/workflows/tests.yml", "CLAUDE.md"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "The image path-filter decision says the old objection is answered, but the filter skips the change class that caused 2 of the 3 measured #386 failures", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. It broke because the rationale describes what the derived check guards (non-source COPY inputs) as if it covered every input. The plan's DECISION (build-plan-ci-on-testament.md) says the earlier objection to a filter (build-plan-server-image.md: it 'would stop catching breaks') 'the derived check now refuses by name', and the supersede note in build-plan-server-image.md says the filter 'cannot fall out of step' with the COPY sources. But tests/test_default_suite_ci_scope.py's _RUN_BY_THE_POOL deliberately exempts arrt/src, arrt/tests and arrt/tools, which are COPY sources, so image.yml does not fire on a source-, test- or tool-only pull request. changes/server-image.md records the image job's first red: two of its three real causes came from changes in exactly that class: a test that found walls.js by a path that holds only in the repository, and tests importing tools/ that the image lacked. The owner reading the DECISION to decide on a veto is told coverage is preserved when it is reduced. deploy/README.md (a subject file) says CI builds the target 'on a pull request that changes what the image is built from', and the image is built from src/tests/tools, so an operator deploying a source-only change believes CI ran the image's suite. Fix: put the residual and its measured base rate in the DECISION and the supersede note (image.yml's header already states it correctly), and make deploy/README.md name what triggers the build (Dockerfile, .dockerignore, interpreter pins, dependency declarations and lockfiles) and say that a source-only change needs `--target test` run by hand or a workflow_dispatch before deploy.", "files": ["deploy/README.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-ci-on-testament.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-server-image.md"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "The per-suite 'nothing was collected' guard was diluted into one merged report, so a suite that selects nothing goes unnoticed", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Cites the learnings rule 'When code is added, moved or retired, ask which guards were scoped to the old shape or to where you looked, and carry them to the surviving code'. assert_tests_ran.py's `tests == 0` check ('The marker expression selected nothing') ran once per browser shard and once per suite job in the retired browser.yml and suites.yml. It now reads one JUnit report merged across all four suites, so it fires only when every suite collects nothing. One suite collecting zero tests passes: for example, the browser suite's `-m browser` matching nothing after a marker or conftest edit, or the contract suite's selection going empty. Governance and curation still report hundreds of tests, so the job stays green while the product's only human-interface coverage runs nothing. Nothing in the repository checks per-suite counts, and if testament's run-level exit 5 / `no_tests` covers one empty suite within a multi-suite run, nothing records that. Search for: every check whose granularity was a job or shard in the retired workflows. Fix it by construction: have the guard (or the summary step, from the envelope) require a non-zero ran count for each suite `.testament.toml` declares, reading the suite names from the manifest so a new suite is covered the day it is added. Prove it fails on a report where one suite is empty.", "files": [".github/scripts/assert_tests_ran.py", ".github/workflows/tests.yml"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "The testament install step exits with no diagnostic when the console's wheel headers are missing", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. In tests.yml's 'Install testament from the pool', `[ -n \"$name\" ] && [ -n \"$want\" ]` under `set -e` ends the step with a bare exit 1 and no message when X-Testament-Wheel-SHA256 is missing. When Content-Disposition is missing, the first test is not the last in the && list, so set -e does not fire, and the step instead dies at `mv wheel.bin \"\"` with an unrelated mv error. headers.txt is in RUNNER_TEMP and is never uploaded, so whoever reads the red check cannot see what the console returned. Every pull request then goes red with no explanation, and the person has to guess between the key, the console address and a changed console response. Replace the line with an explicit check that prints a `::error::` naming which header was absent (and the HTTP status line from headers.txt), then exits 1.", "files": [".github/workflows/tests.yml"], "fid": "R-7"}], "counts": {"blocking": 1, "warning": 6, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "assert_tests_ran's testament xfail match is narrower than testament's own xfail test (fails closed)", "recommendation": "testament's pytest plugin writes a reasonless xfail as `[call] xfail`, with no colon (`_tr_report._skip_text`), and its client's `_is_xfail` accepts that form. `_TESTAMENT_XFAIL = r\"\\[\\w+\\] xfail: \"` does not. Also, junit.py puts the message's LAST non-empty line in the `<skipped message=...>` attribute, so a multi-line xfail reason would lose its `[call] xfail:` opening. Both cases fail the job loudly and name the test, and both of the repo's two xfails have single-line reasons, so nothing is wrong today. If you widen the match, accept the bare `[<phase>] xfail` exactly, and read `skip.text` rather than the attribute.", "files": [".github/scripts/assert_tests_ran.py"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "The image-filter check reads only unindented, single-line COPY; ADD, continuation lines and RUN --mount=type=bind sources escape it", "recommendation": "The plan's DECISION rests on the claim that 'a test derives the Dockerfile's COPY sources so the filter cannot fall out of step'. `_copied_sources` skips `ADD`, any line not starting with `COPY ` (indented or lowercase), and the second and later lines of a backslash-continued COPY. It also skips a `RUN --mount=type=bind,source=...` that reads the context. arrt/Dockerfile has none of these today, so the check is correct for now. To keep the claim true, have the check refuse each unknown form by name (fail on any `ADD`, any COPY ending in `\\`, any bind mount) rather than parse it.", "files": ["tests/test_default_suite_ci_scope.py"], "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "Governing-artifact reconciliation: build-plan-ci-on-testament.md carries no governed_by", "recommendation": "The product has `## Direction` sections (architecture.md and others), and sibling plans such as build-plan-agentic-patterns.md carry `governed_by:`. This plan has none, so I had no disposition to check against the diff. The Direction norms that bear on this change are the public-repo rule (no addresses in the repo) and project-preferences' browser-executed and lint rows. I checked them directly: tests.yml keeps both addresses in repository variables, and the browser row was amended under the recorded 2026-11-18 owner ruling. Seed the field with `prawduct-hook jurisdiction`.", "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "Stale cross-references in test_default_suite_ci_scope.py docstrings", "recommendation": "The docstring of `test_the_pools_curation_suite_selects_every_test_directory_but_the_ones_left_to_others` ends \"as the CI leg's ignores are above\". This commit retired that ignore derivation, so nothing is above. The module docstring says the tests deriving directories from the tree are \"at the end of this file\", and the image-filter tests now come after them. Delete both relational pointers. Prose that nothing reads, so no fix commit is owed for it alone.", "files": ["tests/test_default_suite_ci_scope.py"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "tests.yml's header narrates what CI used to cost", "recommendation": "The sentence \"Before this, a pull request ran four browser shards, the curation suite and a Docker build ... about 51 runner-minutes a push\" is history with a measured figure, and it already lives in build-plan-ci-on-testament.md and the change fragment. A shipped comment should keep the reason (the owner's ruling) and drop the history. Delete the sentence when the file is next touched.", "files": [".github/workflows/tests.yml"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "Load-bearing inline Python in tests.yml, while the repo's convention is a tested script under .github/scripts", "recommendation": "The retry predicate (verdict `unavailable` and `error.retryable`) and the summary/annotation writer are heredoc Python inside YAML, with no test. `assert_tests_ran.py` and `check_drift_freshness.py` are both scripts with suites. The retry fails safe (a misread key means no retry, never a false pass), so this is not a finding. If the envelope handling grows, move both into a `.github/scripts/` helper with a test.", "files": [".github/workflows/tests.yml"], "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "The upload step hard-codes the output directory that every other step derives from OUT_DIR", "recommendation": "The upload-artifact paths spell `${{ runner.temp }}/testament/...`, while the other steps use `$RUNNER_TEMP/$OUT_DIR`. With `if-no-files-found: ignore`, a rename of OUT_DIR would upload nothing and say nothing. Use `${{ runner.temp }}/${{ env.OUT_DIR }}/...`.", "files": [".github/workflows/tests.yml"], "oid": "O-7"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "_copied_sources parses single-line shell-form COPY only", "recommendation": "The Dockerfile has no multi-line, JSON-form or ADD sources today, so the check is complete for now. A `COPY` continued with `\\`, a JSON-array COPY or an `ADD` would drop out of the derived set without failing anything. If the Dockerfile adopts any of these, extend the parser, or make it fail on an unrecognised form.", "files": ["tests/test_default_suite_ci_scope.py"], "oid": "O-8"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Workflow and job names changed with no recorded check that no required status check names them", "recommendation": "The 2026-10-06 sharding change recorded that neither develop nor main had branch protection, so renamed jobs stranded no required check. This change renames or removes every PR check ('Default suites', the browser shards, 'The server image's test target' becoming path-filtered) and the change record does not say the question was re-asked. A required check that never reports blocks merges indefinitely. A one-line confirmation in the change record would close it.", "oid": "O-9"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "The test docstring points at CI-leg ignore tests that this change deleted", "recommendation": "test_the_pools_curation_suite_selects_every_test_directory_but_the_ones_left_to_others's docstring ends 'as the CI leg's ignores are above'. The --ignore derivation tests it refers to were removed with suites.yml. Delete the clause.", "files": ["tests/test_default_suite_ci_scope.py"], "oid": "O-10"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "CLAUDE.md still says the table's commands are what CI runs", "recommendation": "CLAUDE.md line 42 ('The commands above are what CI runs') was true of suites.yml. CI now runs the lint and format columns as written, but runs the tests through `testament run`, which uses .testament.toml's commands (curation with `-n 0 -c arrt/pyproject.toml`, browser included). CLAUDE.md is outside this diff. Making the sentence relational would fix it, for example: 'CI runs the Lint and Format columns, and the tests through testament'.", "oid": "O-11"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Records Pass: oracle set covered", "recommendation": "Rated against the three bars: boundary-patterns.md, build-plan-ci-on-testament.md, build-plan-server-image.md, observability-strategy.md, project-preferences.md, changes/ci-on-testament.md, owner-rulings.md, project-state.yaml. The only record that clears a bar is the image-filter DECISION and its supersede note, folded into the first finding (it misleads the owner's veto into action). The rest are accurate against the diff: the change record's claims about the retry, --no-fallback, the derived COPY check and the retained manifest checks match tests.yml and test_default_suite_ci_scope.py, and the preferences row's claim about what the test holds is true. No edit is wanted there.", "oid": "O-12"}], "duration_seconds": 660, "scope": "ci-on-testament", "scope_chosen_by": "binding", "scope_unresolved_cause": null, "scope_reason": null, "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": ["01"], "chunks_proposed": ["01"], "base_reviewed": "42ce116dbebbe79ef19d86c9f098efe6e2fb7f40", "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/artifacts/boundary-patterns.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-ci-on-testament.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-server-image.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/observability-strategy.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/project-preferences.md", ".prawduct/changes/ci-on-testament.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md", ".prawduct/project-state.yaml", "deploy/README.md", "docs/testing.md"], "chunk_graded": "01", "chunks_graded": ["01"], "plan_graded": ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-ci-on-testament.md", "findings": [], "unchecked": [], "counts": {"chunk-ref-missing": 0, "governed-by-gap": 0, "suite-total-claim": 0, "learnings-over-budget": 0, "learnings-budget-unreaso